316:def compensate_spectral_ols(event_data, spill_matrix, fluoro_indices=None):
317-    """
318-    Compensate spectral event data given a spectral spillover matrix using the
319-    ordinary least squares method (OLS). Unlike conventional flow cytometry
320-    where one detector is used per fluorochrome, spectral flow cytometry utilizes
321-    a larger number of detectors than fluorochromes, resulting in a spectral
322-    matrix that is not square, with more columns than rows. The rows (M) in the
323-    matrix correspond to the "true" fluorochromes (i.e. those detectors with
324-    dedicated fluorochromes). This function assumes the order of the columns
325-    in the matrix is such that the first M columns match the row order, and
326-    the remaining columns are the extra detectors. Also note that because of
327-    the overdetermined system, there is no inverse function for this method of
328-    compensation.
329-
330-    :param event_data: NumPy array of the event data
331-    :param spill_matrix: Compensation matrix as a NumPy array (without headers)
332-    :param fluoro_indices: Optional list of indices of the fluorescent channels (only
333-        these will be extracted & compensated). If None (default), all columns
334-        will be compensated.
335-
336-    :return: NumPy array of compensated event data. If fluoro_indices were given,
337-        the data is returned with the column order given, with the non-fluorescent
338-        columns unmodified.
339-    """
340-    data = event_data.copy()
341-    if fluoro_indices is not None:
342-        comp_data = data[:, fluoro_indices]
343-    else:
344-        comp_data = data
345-
346-    # this does the actual compensation
347-    # The NumPy least squares function returns a tuple of 4 items:
348-    #     least-squares solution (what we want), residuals, rank, & s
349-    # Refer to the NumPy docs for more details. We only want the solution,
350-    # but it needs to be transposed back to our data orientation.
351-    # So, we'll grab the first tuple item and transpose it to avoid storing
352-    # unused variables.
353-    comp_data = np.linalg.lstsq(spill_matrix.T, comp_data.T, rcond=-1)[0].T
